Below is a step-by-step recipe for adding a **hillshade** layer that is driven by a **raster-DEM** source in a MapLibre GL JS map.  
Everything is kept in plain JavaScript (no build tools required) and works with any style you already have – you just add a new source and a new layer.

---

## 1. Prepare a DEM tile service

MapLibre can read any raster tile service that returns **16-bit unsigned integer** elevation values (the same format used by Mapbox Terrain-RGB).  
Typical options:

| Provider | URL template | Notes |
|----------|--------------|-------|
| **MapTiler Terrain** | `https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_KEY` | 16-bit PNG, free tier available |
| **OpenTopoMap DEM** | `https://{s}.tile.opentopomap.org/DEM/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | Public, but limited coverage |
| **Self-hosted GeoTIFF → XYZ** | `https://myserver.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | You can generate tiles with `gdal2tiles.py` (--profile=mercator) |
| **Mapbox Terrain-RGB** (if you have a token) | `https://api.mapbox.com/v4/mapbox.terrain-rgb/{z}/{x}/{y}@2x.pngraw?access_token=YOUR_TOKEN` | 8-bit RGB that encodes elevation; MapLibre can decode it automatically |

> **Important:** The source must be served with CORS headers that allow your page to fetch the tiles (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or your domain).

---

## 2. Add the raster-DEM source to the style

You can either edit the JSON style file directly **or** add the source at runtime with `map.addSource`.  
Below is the runtime approach (easier when you already have a map instance).

```js
// Assuming you already have a MapLibre map instance called `map`
map.addSource('dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  // The URL template from step 1
  url: 'https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_KEY',
  // Optional: set the tile size (default 256). Use 512 for higher-resolution tiles.
  tileSize: 256,
  // Optional: maxzoom of the source (helps the style validator)
  maxzoom: 14,
  // Optional: attribution (good practice)
  attribution: '<a href="https://www.maptiler.com/">MapTiler</a> © OpenMapTiles'
});
```

> **Tip:** If you prefer a static style JSON, add the same block under the `"sources"` object and give it a name (`"dem"` in the example).

---

## 3. Insert a hillshade layer

A hillshade layer is a **type `"hillshade"`**. It reads elevation from the raster-DEM source you just added.

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'dem',          // <-- name of the source we added above
  // Optional: you can limit the layer to a specific zoom range
  minzoom: 0,
  maxzoom: 14,
  // Paint properties – tweak to taste
  paint: {
    // Light direction (azimuth) in degrees clockwise from north
    'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
    // Light intensity (0-1)
    'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',
    // Exaggerate the terrain for a more dramatic effect
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5,
    // Optional colour tweaks
    'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.5)',
    'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)',
    'hillshade-accent-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.2)'
  }
});
```

### What the paint properties do

| Property | Meaning | Typical range |
|----------|---------|---------------|
| `hillshade-illumination-direction` | Sun azimuth (0 = north, 90 = east) | 0-360 |
| `hillshade-illumination-anchor` | `"map"` (sun follows map rotation) or `"viewport"` (sun stays fixed) | `"map"` / `"viewport"` |
| `hillshade-exaggeration` | Multiplies the elevation values – higher = steeper shadows | 0-1 (0 = flat) |
| `hillshade-shadow-color` | Colour of the shadowed side | any CSS colour |
| `hillshade-highlight-color` | Colour of the illuminated side | any CSS colour |
| `hillshade-accent-color` | Colour of the “mid-tone” side (optional) | any CSS colour |

Feel free to experiment – a common “cartographic” look is:

```js
paint: {
  'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
  'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.6,
  'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.5)',
  'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)'
}
```

---

## 4. (Optional) Add a terrain layer for 3-D extrusion

If you also want the map surface itself to be displaced in 3-D (e.g., for a fly-over view), enable **terrain**:

```js
map.setTerrain({
  source: 'dem',
  // Optional: exaggerate the terrain globally
  exaggeration: 1.5
});
```

> **Note:** The hillshade layer works **without** terrain, but the two together give a classic “relief map” look.

---

## 5. Full minimal example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Hillshade from raster-DEM</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style you like
    center: [13.4, 52.5],
    zoom: 6,
    // CJK handling is irrelevant here, but keep the default
    // localIdeographFontFamily: 'sans-serif'
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Add the DEM source
    map.addSource('dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_KEY',
      tileSize: 256,
      maxzoom: 14,
      attribution: '<a href="https://www.maptiler.com/">MapTiler</a> © OpenMapTiles'
    });

    // 2️⃣ Add the hillshade layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.6,
        'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.5)',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)'
      }
    });

    // 3️⃣ (Optional) Enable 3-D terrain
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'dem', exaggeration: 1.2 });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Replace `YOUR_KEY` with your MapTiler (or other provider) API key, or swap the `url` for any other raster-DEM endpoint you control.

---

## 6. Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No hillshade appears** (map stays flat) | DEM source not added *or* `type` is `"raster"` instead of `"raster-dem"` | Ensure `type: 'raster-dem'` and that the source name matches the layer’s `source`. |
| **Gray or white tiles** | DEM tiles are 8-bit PNG (e.g., Mapbox Terrain-RGB) but the source is declared as `"raster-dem"` without `encoding: "mapbox"` | For Terrain-RGB, add `encoding: "mapbox"` to the source definition: <br>`encoding: "mapbox"` |
| **CORS error in console** | Tile server does not send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header | Enable CORS on the server or use a proxy that adds the header. |
| **Hillshade looks blocky** | Using low-resolution tiles (`tileSize: 256` at high zoom) | Serve higher-resolution tiles (`tileSize: 512` or a higher `maxzoom`). |
| **Shadows point the wrong way** | Wrong `hillshade-illumination-direction` value | Adjust the azimuth (0-360°). 315° (north-west) is a common default. |
| **Terrain and hillshade don’t line up** | Different `maxzoom`/`tileSize` between the DEM source used for terrain and the one used for hillshade | Use the *same* source for both (`map.setTerrain({source: 'dem'})`). |

---

## 7. Quick checklist before shipping

1. **Source URL works** – test a single tile in the browser (`…/0/0/0.png`).
2. **CORS headers** – open the network tab, verify `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`.
3. **`type: 'raster-dem'`** – not `'raster'`.
4. **`encoding`** – add `"mapbox"` only for Terrain-RGB; omit for plain 16-bit PNG/GeoTIFF tiles.
5. **Layer order** – hillshade should be **below** any label or symbol layers you want to keep readable. Use `map.moveLayer('hillshade', 'road-label')` or set `before: 'road-label'` when adding.
6. **Performance** – consider limiting the hillshade layer to a max zoom (e.g., `maxzoom: 14`) if you have many high-resolution DEM tiles.

---

### TL;DR

```js
map.addSource('dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_KEY',
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 14
});

map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'dem',
  paint: {
    'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.6,
    'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.5)',
    'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)'
  }
});

// optional 3-D terrain
map.setTerrain({ source: 'dem', exaggeration: 1.2 });
```

That’s all you need to get a crisp, cartographic hillshade on top of any raster-DEM source in MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping!
